.TH AGENT-SANDBOX-DENYLIST 5 "2026-06-30" "agent-sandbox-exec 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
agent-sandbox-denylist \- secret file denylist for agent-sandbox-exec
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file
.I /etc/agent-sandbox/denylist
lists regular files that sandboxed agents (and every process they spawn) must
not read. Opens of these files are denied by the BPF-LSM program with
.BR ENOENT .
.PP
It is watched by
.B sandboxd
and re-applied live \(em no restart is needed after editing.
.SH FORMAT
One path per line:
.PP
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
an absolute path to a
.I regular file
(no directories, no wildcards);
.IP \(bu 2
blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored;
.IP \(bu 2
the file must exist
.RI ( sandboxd
stats each path to resolve its inode; a missing or non-regular path is skipped
with a warning).
.RE
.PP
Matching is by
.I inode
.RI ( {device, inode} ),
so hardlinks and bind-mounts of a listed file are also blocked. An atomic
replace of a secret (a new inode) is caught by the per-file watcher and
re-resolved automatically.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
# /etc/agent-sandbox/denylist
/home/owner/.ssh/id_rsa
/home/owner/.aws/credentials
.fi
.SH CAVEATS
.PP
Only the content read is blocked;
.BR stat (2)
(metadata) of a listed file still succeeds. A process
.I outside
the sandbox opening a secret and passing the fd via a Unix socket can bypass
this scheme.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/agent-sandbox/denylist
The denylist (a dpkg conffile \(em edits survive upgrades).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR agent-sandbox-exec (1),
.BR sandboxd (8).
